  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 212 AM EST Mon Nov 16 2026\n\nEarly morning GOES satellite imagery reveals mostly clear skies \noverhead the Upper Great Lakes as upper level ridging and sfc high \npressure continue to dominate the local weather regime through the \nearly part of the workweek. As the strong upper ridge and sfc high \nbecome established across Appalachia through Thursday and troughing \nslowly lifts across the west coast through the Rockies, SW return \nflow in the Upper Great Lakes will usher in unseasonably warm \ntemperatures to the UP. Daytime highs will easily push the low 50s \neach day, 10-20 degrees above climo normal for mid-November. Lows \nmay not fall below freezing through this time period as well. \nAdditionally, periods of breezy SW winds will be common each day \nthis week. Wed-Thursday, a more put together shortwave emanates out \nof the Rockies, lifting through the High Plains and far western Lake \nSuperior by Thursday night. Isentropic ascent ahead of a developing \nsurface low may be able to touch off some light rain showers across \nthe UP, but ensemble guidance has trended drier with the greatest \nforcing remaining north of the CWA. The latest NBM only suggests a \n~20-50% chance for measurable rainfall by Thursday night, placing \nthe greatest chance in the NW UP.\n\nAttention then turns to the late weekend - early next week period \nwhere operational models are honing in on a cutoff low on the west \ncoast opening up and lifting through the central CONUS, reflecting a \ndeeper surface low feature somewhere through the Upper Great Lakes. \nDeterministic global models and their ensemble counterparts continue \nto highlight temporal and spatial differences in upper level and sfc \nfeature track, timing, and strength, but generally agree upon a \ntrack through MN and western Lake Superior, placing the UP within a \nvast warm sector on Saturday before a sharp cold front quickly \nknocks temperatures down on Sunday/Monday. NW flow behind the \ndeparting system may bring the return of lake effect snow if delta-\nTs and column moisture profiles are favorable. Interested parties \nand Thanksgiving travelers should remain in tune to the forecast. \nWhile a significantly impactful event doesn't look likely at this \ntime, a similar situation occurred last Thanksgiving where a period \nof LES slowly trended toward a major winter storm.",
